{"language":"FCL-1","records":[{"id":"c1","type":"claim","text":"The recurring disputes are about what an agreement meant rather than about whether a task was done, which indicates the agreements are underspecified: they fix who does something but leave frequency, standard and deadline open, and each party later reads the gaps in their own favor. This is a conjecture from the asker's wording, not an established fact.","scope":"Ordinary household chores in this three-person flat.","grounds":"The asker's description: they agree on who will do something but later disagree about what the agreement meant.","mentions":["h005.task.v1#aceac4253907a8e6"]},{"id":"c2","type":"claim","text":"Because the residents' work schedules change, any fixed allocation of chores decays into unfairness over time without anyone acting in bad faith; a one-time settlement therefore cannot hold, and the household needs a periodic renewal mechanism rather than a single negotiated split.","grounds":"The asker's report that work schedules change.","mentions":["h005.task.v1#aceac4253907a8e6"]},{"id":"c3","type":"claim","text":"Recurring avoidance of the chore conversations is most plausibly maintained by the conversations having become aversive; a conversation dominated by retrospective dispute over what was agreed is predictably aversive. On this reading the avoidance is a symptom of the conversation format, not an independent defect in the friend.","scope":"The one friend described as avoiding the conversations.","grounds":"The described pattern of later disagreements about agreements, plus general features of maintained avoidance; held as conjecture.","mentions":["h005.task.v1#aceac4253907a8e6"]},{"id":"c4","type":"claim","text":"In a three-person household, a group conversation about chores can take on a two-on-one character for whichever resident is least aligned with the other two, so that it feels like judgment rather than planning; this is a plausible contributor to the avoidance, though unverified.","grounds":"Structural feature of triads; conjectural application to this household."},{"id":"k1","type":"commitment","text":"Propose that when a chore agreement is made, the person taking the task records it in a shared place (group chat or shared note) in their own words, including what the task covers and how often or by when.","action":"Introduce doer-worded written capture of chore agreements; the asker can begin unilaterally with their own undertakings.","scope":"Ordinary chores in this flat.","consequence":"Later disputes about meaning become checkable against a sentence the doer wrote themselves, which is harder to reinterpret than a spoken agreement.","depends":["c1"]},{"id":"k2","type":"commitment","text":"Propose a short, recurring, scheduled check-in (roughly fifteen minutes, fortnightly) with a fixed agenda: what did not work since last time, and what needs adjusting for the coming period given schedule changes.","action":"Establish a routine check-in in place of ad hoc negotiation.","consequence":"Disputes move off the trigger path — a disagreement at the moment a task comes due becomes a scheduled item — and schedule drift gains a standing venue for correction.","depends":["c2","c3"]},{"id":"k3","type":"commitment","text":"Propose that the asker approach the avoiding friend one-on-one, in a low-stakes setting, asking what would make the chore arrangements easier for them — framed as a question, not as a summons back into the group conversation.","action":"One-on-one, non-accusatory inquiry before any group-format pressure.","consequence":"Tests the cause of the avoidance without the two-on-one dynamic; forcing the group format on someone already avoiding it is likely to deepen the avoidance.","depends":["c3","c4"],"target":["p1"]},{"id":"o1","type":"objection","text":"Against pushing the written-agreement and check-in measures into full formalization (detailed rotas, cleaning standards, tracking apps): these are friends, not parties to a contract, and past some point explicitness reads as enforcement between friends, which can damage the relationship the arrangements are meant to protect.","target":["k1","k2"],"bearing":"This limits how far k1 and k2 should go — the measures should stay light, revisable and droppable if they start to feel heavy; it does not defeat the measures themselves.","scope":"Degree of formalization, not the existence of the measures."},{"id":"o2","type":"objection","text":"The account is built entirely from one participant's framing. It is consistent with the asker's description that the friends experience the asker as relitigating past agreements with heavy, prosecutorial energy, and that the avoidance is partly a response to that. Remedies that assume the asker is a neutral coordinator may fail for this reason.","target":["c1","c3","k1","k2","k3"],"bearing":"The first test of any proposed remedy is asking the other two residents what they think the problem is; the asker should hold their own reading of the disputes loosely.","mentions":["h005.task.v1#aceac4253907a8e6"]},{"id":"p1","type":"problem","text":"The cause of the friend's avoidance cannot be determined from the asker's description. At least two opposite explanations fit — feeling unfairly blamed or outnumbered, or dodging obligations the friend knows are unmet — and they call for different responses. Resolving this requires input the account does not have.","mentions":["c3","c4"]},{"id":"p2","type":"problem","text":"Where the line sits between enough explicitness to prevent disputes and so much that coordination feels like contract enforcement among friends is household-specific and cannot be settled from the asker's description; it will have to be found by trying the lighter measures first and watching how they land.","target":["k1","k2"],"mentions":["o1"]}],"uptake":["c1","c2","c3","c4","k1","k2","k3","o1","o2","p1","p2"]}